              "transcript": "Part two of a delusional girl being quoted £30,000 for her dressing room of dreams and deciding that she can do it all by herself. If you missed part one, we did DIY flooring and now we're on to built-ins on a budget. These are the IKEA packed wardrobes and this was actually the easiest part because these literally, they just fold out. You don't actually need to put them together. You just lift them into place and this sums up how difficult my husband thought it was. Now we want that inbuilt bespoke look. So I am making a timber frame out of 6x2 timber and I'm making this to the exact dimensions of my wardrobe frames adding additional filler pieces where the wardrobes join secured it all together and then you can literally just lift your wardrobes on top and then to really give it that bespoke look I picked up some sheets of MDF in B&Q and they will actually cut this for you for 50p to the exact size you need you need some for your side panels you need some for underneath your wardrobes and then you need some on top so that it goes completely flush from the floor to the ceiling and then adding the skirting board this is going to go along the front of my wardrobes and on the walls too to really give it that inbuilt look and that is pretty much where i'm at for now i have just started to work on the doors i want to add moldings to make them look really intricate and i think i've decided to do a two panel design so a smaller one at the bottom that goes in line with the dressing table and then a bigger one at the top and i think it's going to look so good one door down about 20 to go i'm going to add some coving the whole way around the room which i think will look spectacular i need to find the perfect of worktop for my dressing table and then that pile of stuff in the middle will hopefully become an island so make sure you follow along for part three to see if I achieve my dressing room of dreams on a budget.",
